append_md('SP61_CW_75_notes.md',r'''## Scan 021

The opening finishes the extension-of-group argument from scan 020. Item ⑥ concerns products of torsors for a commutative group: associativity, commutativity, a unit, closure among torsors, and an inverse obtained from the negation homomorphism. Item ⑦ revises an example into “Opération X∧Y entre ens. à 2 élémts.” The final boxed and cancelled example has a finite set partitioned into two-element classes and an indexed product of those classes. No date or authorial page count; pencil archive number 20.

**Mathematics and reading.** In the opening, extension is written as `T^{G′}` with a cancelled G′ on the left. The twist of F is then written on the right as `F^T`; it is not standardized to the earlier left-twist notation. The blackened superscript on Aut(F) is not made into H’s live circle. One cancelled word before que remains physically unreadable.

The commutative hypothesis explains the sentence “Op. à g ou à droite pareil,” but does not change the direction words on scan 020. A direct balanced-product check is recorded as S021-PRODUCT. The inverse proposition ends “tel que.” without stating the compatibility condition. Uniqueness requires a chosen compatibility with the displayed trivializations; S021-INVERSE checks that qualified formulation, not an unqualified unique isomorphism between arbitrary torsors. The inverse formula’s two connectors are blunt in the crop, including the source-barred connector in the label g to −g.

The unit has a double stem. At its second occurrence it overwrites a G-like symbol; H’s plain underlined G loses the correction. The wording near the first unit is still doubtful (pour/joue role d’une unité), and no a or la is supplied to improve it. The last example is all cancelled, while the small new two-element-set example above is live. These layers, rather than a completed mathematical exposition, are what is transcribed.''')
append_md('SP61_CW_75_doubts.md',r'''### Scan 021 — certainty-only decisions

The crossed initial exponent of T is G′, the cancelled example starts Si, and the later cancelled word is disons,. Between the propositions the crossed word is Soit without a separate Y. The first unit is a double-stem 1. The one-stroke continuation in the general product is a dash. The mark above the final big wedge has multiple projections and is read as an asterisk; H already describes it in a note. The blackened word before que remains illegible even in its crop. “déduite,” “Pour un autre,” and both isom abbreviations are readable.
''')
append_md('SP61_CW_75_hand_log.md',r'''## Added on scan 021

- **Unit notation has two stems and a base.** The first 1 is double-stemmed; the next is written over an earlier G-like unit and separately underlined. H keeps only the earlier G in the proposition. This distinction is confined to the two unit signs on this page.
- **Source-barred connectors may end bluntly.** The raised g-to-minus-g label again has a transverse end; the underlying G-to-G shaft has a bead-like end. Pointed arrows elsewhere are still read separately.
- **Heavy cancellation does not imply every nearby word is cancelled.** The live section-7 insertion runs above the old sentence and continues below it. H treats much of the new example as cancelled. The overwritten X in the product shorthand was instead omitted by C.
- **Some blots cannot be recovered.** The word before que has no stable letter contour even after enlargement; it remains an illegible one-word deletion. This is not used as a model for the readable crossed disons or Soit.
''')
checks([('S021-EXTENSION','used and rechecked in this run','The opening is the torsor specialization of S020-ASSOC: twisting a G′-object by the extended torsor T×_G G′ gives its twist by T after restriction along G→G′. The check does not regularize the side on which the source writes its twist exponents.'),('S021-PRODUCT','targeted proof check','For abelian G and G-torsors X,Y, use the balanced relation (x+g,y)~(x,y+g). Choosing origins identifies the quotient with G by the sum of coordinates, proving it is a torsor. For three factors the same total-coordinate map proves associativity, swapping factors proves commutativity, and X×_G G→X is the unit map. These descriptions descend intrinsically, so the checks do not depend on the temporary origins.'),('S021-INVERSE','targeted proof check','Give the underlying set of X the opposite action to obtain X^{-1}; the difference of two points gives X∧X^{-1}≅G. If Y and Y′ carry specified trivializations X∧Y≅G and X∧Y′≅G, exactly one equivariant Y→Y′ respects them: in coordinates it is the unique translation cancelling the difference of the two trivializations. Without that compatibility, torsor automorphisms prevent uniqueness. The manuscript leaves “tel que” unfinished.'),('S021-TWO','targeted proof check','For two two-element sets with their canonical free C2-actions, the diagonal flip on the four ordered pairs has two orbits. Thus their balanced product is again a two-element set. The larger boxed example is only an unfinished cancelled setup and is not claimed proved.')])
